# A Guided Tour of Reflective Type Theory

A proposition can describe the world, but a reflective proposition can also describe the availability of its own evidence. This tour begins with binding, moves through a finite semantic witness, and ends with an exact bridge to modal fixed-point logic. For background, see the [modal logic overview](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-modal/) and the [modal μ-calculus overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modal_%CE%BC-calculus).

## 1. Safe self-reference

The recursive expression $\mu X.\Box X$ says, informally, “the proposition obtained by asserting my own provability.” Its variable is safe because it occurs under a binder. Change the binder depth and index interactively:

{{interactive_demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the binding definition and proof idea</summary>
A de Bruijn variable with index $i$ is legal under $n$ binders exactly when $i<n$. Entering $\mu$ raises $n$ by one; $\Box$ leaves it unchanged. In $\mu X.\Box X$, index $0$ is checked at depth $1$, so $0<1$ and the whole expression is closed.
</details>

{{algorithm:0}}

Run the compact numerical example:

{{demo:0}}

## 2. One box need not equal two

The statement $\Box A\wedge
eg\Box\Box A$ separates immediate certification from certification that survives another accessible step. The following frame makes the geometry visible:

{{visualization:0}}

Explore valuations on the chain and watch repeated boxing change the truth set:

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal why transitivity forbids the witness</summary>
If $wRv$ and $vRu$ always imply $wRu$, then $w\models\Box A$ forces every two-step endpoint $u$ to satisfy $A$. Consequently every successor $v$ satisfies $\Box A$, giving $w\models\Box\Box A$. Thus $\Box A\wedge
eg\Box\Box A$ requires failure of transitivity.
</details>

{{visualization:1}}

## 3. A proper extension

The ordinary language has atoms, empty and unit types, products, and arrows. Reflection adds bound variables, boxes, and fixed points without identifying any old expressions.

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the strictness argument</summary>
The structural inclusion preserves every old outer constructor and is therefore injective. Yet no included ordinary type can equal $\Box a$, because no old translation clause creates an outer box. The extension is faithful and genuinely larger.
</details>

## 4. The modal fixed-point bridge

Every type constructor has a modal counterpart: product becomes conjunction, arrow becomes implication, empty becomes falsity, unit becomes truth, and box and fixed point remain box and fixed point.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the inverse-translation theorem</summary>
Structural induction shows that translating a reflective type to a modal formula and back returns the original type. A symmetric induction proves the other round trip. Since variables and binders are copied exactly, the correspondence preserves scoping as well as tree shape.
</details>

## 5. Continue exploring

The next questions concern positivity, monotone fixed points, finite convergence bounds, substitution, and whether positive introspection characterizes transitive frames. The [Knaster–Tarski theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knaster%E2%80%93Tarski_theorem) provides the natural fixed-point background for those developments.

